# tramaj

A small template language for turning a JSON value into a document tree.

Templates are meant to be comfortable to write by hand *and* easy for an LLM
to emit: a HAML-like block syntax for structure, a `jq`-flavoured expression
language for the data half, and a deliberately tiny builtin set. Rendering is
decoupled from the language — evaluation produces a generic `Node` AST, and
it is the host that decides whether that becomes Halogen HTML, JSON, or
anything else.

```
@items=$ctx.items
@n=cardinality($items)
.div(class: "items",
  .h1("Items"),
  .p("there are `$n` of them"),
  .ul(map($items, (i) => .li(action("on-click", "select", {"id": $i.id}), $i.name))))
```

Bindings are evaluated once, in order. `` `$n` `` interpolates into a
string; a bare `$path` can be used directly as a child. `action(...)`
attaches an opaque, structured action to an element — the language
recognises the syntax but assigns it no meaning, leaving the host to map the
key onto a real event handler.

Go straight to
[`specs/reference.md`](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/tramaj/blob/main/specs/reference.md)
on GitHub for the full spec, or explore the site below.

## Explore

- [**Properties**](/properties.html) — the invariants tramaj is held to:
  portability, cheap static analysis, determinism.
- [**Use cases**](/use-cases.html) — where a small document-composing
  language fits: static sites, real UIs, LLM-generated templates,
  constraint-driven and typed generation.
- [**Reference**](/reference.html) — the language specification, the `Node`
  wire format, and the v3/v4 extensions.
- [**Playground**](/playground.html) — try it in the browser, nothing sent
  to a server.
- [**Getting started**](/howto-humans.html) — install the toolchain, write
  and run a first template.
- [**Integrating tramaj**](/integrate-tramaj.html) — embedding it as a
  library in PureScript, Haskell, JavaScript, or anything else.
- [**For coding agents**](/howto-agents.html) — a dense syntax cheatsheet
  and fetch strategy for an LLM emitting tramaj templates.
- [**Roadmap**](/roadmap.html) — where the implementations stand, and the
  native JS, Python, and Rust implementations that are wanted next.

